1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,202
Children are a strange people; they appear
in dreams and in visions.

2
00:00:04,222 --> 00:00:07,369
Before Christmas, and right up to
Christmas itself,

3
00:00:07,389 --> 00:00:11,405
I kept meeting one little boy on the
street, at a certain corner,

4
00:00:11,425 --> 00:00:13,206
no more than seven years old.

5
00:00:13,226 --> 00:00:16,621
In the terrible frost he was dressed
almost for summer,

6
00:00:16,641 --> 00:00:19,167
yet his neck was wrapped in some old rag,

7
00:00:19,187 --> 00:00:22,396
so somebody had still equipped him and
sent him out.

8
00:00:22,416 --> 00:00:26,122
He went about “with his hand out”; that is
a technical term,

9
00:00:26,142 --> 00:00:27,177
it means begging.

10
00:00:27,197 --> 00:00:29,537
The boys themselves invented the term.

11
00:00:29,557 --> 00:00:31,524
There are a great many like him;

12
00:00:31,544 --> 00:00:35,746
they swarm across your path and whine
something they have learned by

13
00:00:35,766 --> 00:00:37,857
heart. But this one did not whine.

14
00:00:37,877 --> 00:00:40,776
He spoke somehow innocently and
unaccustomedly,

15
00:00:40,796 --> 00:00:42,949
and looked trustfully into my eyes,

16
00:00:42,969 --> 00:00:45,743
so he was evidently only beginning the
trade.

17
00:00:45,763 --> 00:00:50,152
To my questions he answered that he had a
sister, out of work and sick.

18
00:00:50,172 --> 00:00:51,394
Perhaps it was true;

19
00:00:51,414 --> 00:00:55,865
but I learned afterwards that there are
countless numbers of these boys:

20
00:00:55,885 --> 00:00:59,963
they are sent out “with the hand” even in
the most dreadful frost,

21
00:00:59,983 --> 00:01:03,564
and if they collect nothing, a beating
surely awaits them.

22
00:01:03,584 --> 00:01:07,104
Having gathered his kopecks, the boy comes
back with red,

23
00:01:07,124 --> 00:01:11,326
numbed hands to some cellar where a gang
of smock-wearing workmen is

24
00:01:11,346 --> 00:01:13,003
drinking, the very men who,

25
00:01:13,023 --> 00:01:16,977
“having gone on strike at the factory on
Saturday before Sunday,

26
00:01:16,997 --> 00:01:20,019
return to work no earlier than Wednesday
evening.

27
00:01:20,039 --> 00:01:23,993
There in the cellars their hungry, beaten
wives drink with them,

28
00:01:24,013 --> 00:01:26,663
and right there their hungry babies
squeal.

29
00:01:26,683 --> 00:01:30,079
Vodka, and filth, and debauchery, and
above all, vodka.

30
00:01:30,099 --> 00:01:34,549
With the kopecks he has gathered, the boy
is sent at once to the tavern,

31
00:01:34,569 --> 00:01:36,412
and he brings back more drink.

32
00:01:36,432 --> 00:01:39,827
For fun they sometimes pour a glass into
his mouth too,

33
00:01:39,847 --> 00:01:43,243
and roar with laughter when, with his
breath cut short,

34
00:01:43,263 --> 00:01:46,782
he falls almost senseless to the floor,
and they pour on,

35
00:01:47,552 --> 00:01:51,612
and into my mouth the foul vodka, without
pity they poured...

36
00:01:52,382 --> 00:01:54,171
When he grows a little older,

37
00:01:54,191 --> 00:01:57,726
they pack him off as quickly as they can
to some factory,

38
00:01:57,746 --> 00:02:01,780
but everything he earns he is bound to
bring back to the workmen,

39
00:02:01,800 --> 00:02:03,277
who drink it away again.

40
00:02:03,297 --> 00:02:07,581
But even before the factory these children
become complete criminals.

41
00:02:07,601 --> 00:02:12,009
They wander about the town and know places
in various cellars where one

42
00:02:12,029 --> 00:02:14,691
can crawl in and spend the night
unnoticed.

43
00:02:14,711 --> 00:02:18,870
One of them slept several nights running
in a basket belonging to a

44
00:02:18,890 --> 00:02:22,051
certain yard-keeper, and the man never
noticed him.

45
00:02:22,071 --> 00:02:24,358
Naturally they become little thieves.

46
00:02:24,378 --> 00:02:28,225
Stealing turns into a passion even in
eight-year-old children,

47
00:02:28,245 --> 00:02:31,718
sometimes without any awareness that the
act is a crime.

48
00:02:31,738 --> 00:02:35,336
In the end they endure everything, hunger,
cold, beatings,

49
00:02:35,356 --> 00:02:37,082
for one thing only, freedom,

50
00:02:37,102 --> 00:02:40,824
and they run away from their workmen to
wander on their own.

51
00:02:40,844 --> 00:02:44,317
This wild creature sometimes understands
nothing at all:

52
00:02:44,337 --> 00:02:47,373
neither where he lives, nor of what nation
he is,

53
00:02:47,393 --> 00:02:50,866
nor whether there is a God, nor whether
there is a Tsar.

54
00:02:50,886 --> 00:02:54,546
Such things are told about them that it is
hard to believe,

55
00:02:54,566 --> 00:02:56,230
and yet they are all facts.

56
00:02:58,750 --> 00:03:03,210
But I am a novelist, and it seems I have
made up one “story” myself.

57
00:03:03,230 --> 00:03:04,924
Why do I write “it seems”?

58
00:03:04,944 --> 00:03:07,230
I know very well that I made it up;

59
00:03:07,250 --> 00:03:11,051
yet I keep imagining that it happened
somewhere, sometime,

60
00:03:11,071 --> 00:03:14,082
that it happened just on the eve of
Christmas,

61
00:03:14,102 --> 00:03:17,113
in some enormous city and in a terrible
frost.

62
00:03:17,883 --> 00:03:21,519
I imagine there was a boy in a cellar, but
still very small,

63
00:03:21,539 --> 00:03:23,165
six years old or even less.

64
00:03:23,185 --> 00:03:26,516
This boy woke up in the morning in a damp,
cold cellar.

65
00:03:26,536 --> 00:03:30,295
He was dressed in some sort of little
smock and was shivering.

66
00:03:30,315 --> 00:03:34,621
His breath flew out as white steam, and
sitting in a corner on a chest,

67
00:03:34,641 --> 00:03:38,400
he blew the steam out of his mouth on
purpose, out of boredom,

68
00:03:38,420 --> 00:03:40,959
and amused himself by watching it fly out.

69
00:03:40,979 --> 00:03:42,848
But he very much wanted to eat.

70
00:03:42,868 --> 00:03:47,053
Several times since morning he had gone up
to the bunk where his sick

71
00:03:47,073 --> 00:03:49,795
mother lay on a bedding as thin as a
pancake,

72
00:03:49,815 --> 00:03:52,537
with some bundle under her head for a
pillow.

73
00:03:52,557 --> 00:03:54,244
How had she come to be here?

74
00:03:54,264 --> 00:03:58,510
She must have arrived with her boy from
some strange town and suddenly

75
00:03:58,530 --> 00:03:59,180
fallen ill.

76
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:03,385
The woman who kept the lodgings had been
taken to the police two days

77
00:04:03,405 --> 00:04:06,554
before; the lodgers had scattered, it was
a holiday,

78
00:04:06,574 --> 00:04:10,941
and the one workman who was left had
already lain dead drunk for a whole

79
00:04:10,961 --> 00:04:14,049
day, not having waited even for the
holiday itself.

80
00:04:14,069 --> 00:04:17,401
In another corner of the room an eighty-
year-old woman,

81
00:04:17,421 --> 00:04:21,301
who had once lived somewhere as a nurse
and was now dying alone,

82
00:04:21,321 --> 00:04:25,445
groaned with rheumatism, sighing,
grumbling and growling at the boy,

83
00:04:25,465 --> 00:04:28,979
so that he had come to be afraid of going
near her corner.

84
00:04:28,999 --> 00:04:32,331
A drink of water he had found somewhere in
the passage,

85
00:04:32,351 --> 00:04:34,403
but a crust he could find nowhere,

86
00:04:34,423 --> 00:04:37,815
and for the tenth time already he went to
wake his mama.

87
00:04:37,835 --> 00:04:42,203
At last he began to feel uneasy in the
dark: evening had begun long ago,

88
00:04:42,223 --> 00:04:43,483
and no light was lit.

89
00:04:43,503 --> 00:04:45,006
Touching his mama's face,

90
00:04:45,026 --> 00:04:49,333
he was surprised that she did not move at
all and had become as cold as

91
00:04:49,353 --> 00:04:53,416
the wall. “It is very cold here,” he
thought, stood a little while,

92
00:04:53,436 --> 00:04:57,255
unconsciously forgetting his hand on the
dead woman's shoulder,

93
00:04:57,275 --> 00:05:00,668
then breathed on his fingers to warm them,
and suddenly,

94
00:05:00,688 --> 00:05:05,116
groping for his little cap on the bunk, he
went quietly, feeling his way,

95
00:05:05,136 --> 00:05:06,213
out of the cellar.

96
00:05:06,233 --> 00:05:08,163
He would have gone even earlier,

97
00:05:08,183 --> 00:05:12,551
but upstairs on the staircase he was
afraid of a big dog that had howled

98
00:05:12,571 --> 00:05:14,501
all day by the neighbours' door.

99
00:05:14,521 --> 00:05:18,340
But the dog was gone, and he suddenly came
out into the street.

100
00:05:20,860 --> 00:05:21,850
Lord, what a city!

101
00:05:21,870 --> 00:05:23,812
He had never seen anything like it.

102
00:05:23,832 --> 00:05:27,121
Where he had come from, the nights are
such black darkness,

103
00:05:27,141 --> 00:05:28,692
one lamp for a whole street.

104
00:05:28,712 --> 00:05:32,001
Low wooden houses are shut up with
shutters; in the street,

105
00:05:32,021 --> 00:05:34,132
as soon as dusk falls there is nobody,

106
00:05:34,152 --> 00:05:38,114
everyone shuts himself in his house, and
only whole packs of dogs howl,

107
00:05:38,134 --> 00:05:41,591
hundreds and thousands of them, howling
and barking all night.

108
00:05:41,611 --> 00:05:45,573
But there it was so warm, and they gave
him something to eat, and here,

109
00:05:45,593 --> 00:05:47,087
Lord, if only he could eat!

110
00:05:47,107 --> 00:05:50,452
And what a clatter and thunder here, what
light, and people,

111
00:05:50,472 --> 00:05:53,088
horses and carriages, and the frost, the
frost!

112
00:05:53,108 --> 00:05:55,444
Frozen steam pours from the driven horses,

113
00:05:55,464 --> 00:05:57,407
from their hotly breathing muzzles;

114
00:05:57,427 --> 00:06:00,604
through the loose snow the horseshoes ring
on the stones,

115
00:06:00,624 --> 00:06:03,969
and everyone jostles so, and, Lord, he
wants so much to eat,

116
00:06:03,989 --> 00:06:07,782
just a morsel of something, and his little
fingers suddenly hurt so.

117
00:06:07,802 --> 00:06:11,484
A policeman passed by and turned away so
as not to notice the boy.

118
00:06:12,254 --> 00:06:14,615
And here is another street, oh, how wide!

119
00:06:14,635 --> 00:06:18,448
Here they will surely crush him; how they
all shout, run and ride,

120
00:06:18,468 --> 00:06:19,900
and the light, the light!

121
00:06:19,920 --> 00:06:20,887
And what is this?

122
00:06:20,907 --> 00:06:24,255
Oh, what a big pane of glass, and behind
the glass a room,

123
00:06:24,275 --> 00:06:28,030
and in the room a tree up to the ceiling;
it is a Christmas tree,

124
00:06:28,050 --> 00:06:31,805
and on the tree how many lights, how many
gold papers and apples,

125
00:06:31,825 --> 00:06:36,102
and all around little dolls, tiny horses;
and children run about the room,

126
00:06:36,122 --> 00:06:38,425
dressed up, clean, laughing and playing,

127
00:06:38,445 --> 00:06:40,399
and eating and drinking something.

128
00:06:40,419 --> 00:06:44,000
Here a girl has begun to dance with a boy,
what a pretty girl!

129
00:06:44,020 --> 00:06:47,136
And there is music, you can hear it
through the glass.

130
00:06:47,156 --> 00:06:49,691
The boy looks, marvels, is already
laughing,

131
00:06:49,711 --> 00:06:53,814
but his little fingers already hurt, both
on his feet and on his hands,

132
00:06:53,834 --> 00:06:57,705
which have turned quite red, will no
longer bend, and hurt to move.

133
00:06:57,725 --> 00:07:01,422
And suddenly the boy remembered that his
little fingers hurt so,

134
00:07:01,442 --> 00:07:05,429
began to cry and ran on, and now he sees
through another pane a room,

135
00:07:05,449 --> 00:07:09,320
again there are trees, but on the tables
there are pies, all kinds,

136
00:07:09,340 --> 00:07:13,094
almond ones, red, yellow, and four rich
ladies are sitting there,

137
00:07:13,114 --> 00:07:17,159
and whoever comes in they give a pie, and
the door opens every minute,

138
00:07:17,179 --> 00:07:20,121
and many gentlemen come in to them from
the street.

139
00:07:20,141 --> 00:07:23,315
The boy crept up, suddenly opened the door
and went in.

140
00:07:23,335 --> 00:07:25,522
Oh, how they shouted at him and waved!

141
00:07:25,542 --> 00:07:29,355
One lady quickly came up and thrust a
little kopeck into his hand,

142
00:07:29,375 --> 00:07:32,258
and herself opened the door to the street
for him.

143
00:07:32,278 --> 00:07:33,536
How frightened he was!

144
00:07:33,556 --> 00:07:37,078
And the little kopeck rolled right out and
rang on the steps:

145
00:07:37,098 --> 00:07:40,098
he could not bend his red fingers and hold
on to it.

146
00:07:40,118 --> 00:07:44,338
The boy ran out and went quickly, quickly,
where he did not know himself.

147
00:07:44,358 --> 00:07:46,486
He wants to cry again, but is afraid,

148
00:07:46,506 --> 00:07:48,867
and runs and runs and blows on his hands.

149
00:07:48,887 --> 00:07:52,932
And misery seizes him, because he suddenly
feels so lonely and uneasy,

150
00:07:52,952 --> 00:07:54,036
and suddenly, Lord!

151
00:07:54,056 --> 00:07:55,139
What is this again?

152
00:07:55,159 --> 00:07:57,172
People stand in a crowd marvelling:

153
00:07:57,192 --> 00:08:00,308
in a window behind glass there are three
dolls, small,

154
00:08:00,328 --> 00:08:04,257
dressed in little red and green frocks and
quite, quite like living!

155
00:08:04,277 --> 00:08:06,986
An old man sits and seems to play a big
violin,

156
00:08:07,006 --> 00:08:09,715
two others stand there and play little
violins,

157
00:08:09,735 --> 00:08:13,142
and nod their little heads in time, and
look at each other,

158
00:08:13,162 --> 00:08:15,813
and their lips move, they speak, really
speak,

159
00:08:15,833 --> 00:08:18,078
only through the glass you cannot hear.

160
00:08:18,098 --> 00:08:20,691
And at first the boy thought they were
alive,

161
00:08:20,711 --> 00:08:24,931
and when he guessed that they were dolls,
he suddenly burst out laughing.

162
00:08:24,951 --> 00:08:28,705
Never had he seen such dolls and he did
not know there were such!

163
00:08:28,725 --> 00:08:32,422
He wants to cry, but it is so funny, so
funny, the little dolls.

164
00:08:32,442 --> 00:08:36,487
Suddenly he felt that someone had seized
him from behind by his little

165
00:08:36,507 --> 00:08:36,835
smock:

166
00:08:36,855 --> 00:08:40,959
a big wicked boy stood beside him and
suddenly cracked him on the head,

167
00:08:40,979 --> 00:08:44,385
tore off his cap, and from below tripped
him with his foot.

168
00:08:44,405 --> 00:08:48,044
The boy rolled to the ground, there was a
shout, he went faint,

169
00:08:48,064 --> 00:08:51,876
jumped up and ran and ran, and suddenly
ran he did not know where,

170
00:08:51,896 --> 00:08:53,967
into a gateway, into a strange yard,

171
00:08:53,987 --> 00:08:57,858
and crouched down behind the firewood:
“They will not find me here,

172
00:08:57,878 --> 00:08:58,787
and it is dark.”

173
00:09:01,307 --> 00:09:05,528
He crouched down and curled up, unable to
catch his breath for fear,

174
00:09:05,548 --> 00:09:09,021
and suddenly, quite suddenly, it became so
good for him:

175
00:09:09,041 --> 00:09:13,575
his hands and feet suddenly stopped
hurting and it grew so warm, so warm,

176
00:09:13,595 --> 00:09:16,818
as on top of a stove; now he shuddered all
over: ah,

177
00:09:16,838 --> 00:09:19,126
but he had been about to fall asleep!

178
00:09:19,146 --> 00:09:21,309
How good it is to fall asleep here:

179
00:09:21,329 --> 00:09:25,488
“I will sit here a little and then go and
look at the dolls again,”

180
00:09:25,508 --> 00:09:29,792
thought the boy, and smiled, remembering
them, “quite like living!...

181
00:09:29,812 --> 00:09:33,659
And suddenly he heard his mama singing a
little song over him.

182
00:09:33,679 --> 00:09:37,027
“Mama, I am asleep, ah, how good it is to
sleep here!”

183
00:09:37,797 --> 00:09:39,546
“Come to my Christmas tree,

184
00:09:39,566 --> 00:09:43,150
little boy,” a quiet voice suddenly
whispered over him.

185
00:09:43,920 --> 00:09:48,255
He thought at first that it was still his
mama, but no, it was not she;

186
00:09:48,275 --> 00:09:50,770
who it was that called him he cannot see,

187
00:09:50,790 --> 00:09:54,389
but someone bent over him and embraced him
in the darkness,

188
00:09:54,409 --> 00:09:57,026
and he stretched out his hand to him
and...

189
00:09:57,046 --> 00:09:58,805
and suddenly, oh, what light!

190
00:09:58,825 --> 00:09:59,787
Oh, what a tree!

191
00:09:59,807 --> 00:10:03,835
And it is not even a Christmas tree, he
has never seen such trees!

192
00:10:03,855 --> 00:10:04,816
Where is he now?

193
00:10:04,836 --> 00:10:09,294
Everything glitters, everything shines,
and all around are dolls, but no,

194
00:10:09,314 --> 00:10:13,710
these are all boys and girls, only so
bright, they all whirl around him,

195
00:10:13,730 --> 00:10:17,023
fly, they all kiss him, take him, carry
him with them,

196
00:10:17,043 --> 00:10:19,108
and he himself flies, and he sees:

197
00:10:19,128 --> 00:10:22,543
his mama is looking at him and laughing at
him joyfully.

198
00:10:23,313 --> 00:10:25,309
“Mama! Mama! Ah, how good it is here,
Mama!

199
00:10:25,329 --> 00:10:27,889
the boy cries to her, and again he kisses
the children,

200
00:10:27,909 --> 00:10:30,984
and wants to tell them quickly about those
dolls behind the glass.

201
00:10:31,004 --> 00:10:31,875
“Who are you, boys?

202
00:10:31,895 --> 00:10:32,766
Who are you, girls?

203
00:10:32,786 --> 00:10:34,360
he asks, laughing and loving them.

204
00:10:35,130 --> 00:10:37,599
“This is Christ's tree,” they answer him.

205
00:10:37,619 --> 00:10:42,031
“Christ always has a tree on this day for
the little children who have no

206
00:10:42,051 --> 00:10:43,609
tree of their own there...

207
00:10:43,629 --> 00:10:47,920
And he learned that these boys and girls
were all just such children as

208
00:10:47,940 --> 00:10:50,894
he, but some had frozen already in their
baskets,

209
00:10:50,914 --> 00:10:55,205
in which they had been left on the stairs
at the doors of the officials

210
00:10:55,225 --> 00:10:59,455
of Petersburg; others had been smothered
in the care of Finnish women,

211
00:10:59,475 --> 00:11:02,186
put out to nurse from the foundling
hospital;

212
00:11:02,206 --> 00:11:05,647
others had died at the dried-up breasts of
their mothers,

213
00:11:05,667 --> 00:11:07,165
during the Samara famine;

214
00:11:07,185 --> 00:11:11,475
others had suffocated in third-class
railway carriages from the stench;

215
00:11:11,495 --> 00:11:15,057
and now they are all here, all of them now
are like angels,

216
00:11:15,077 --> 00:11:18,457
all with Christ, and He Himself is in the
midst of them,

217
00:11:18,477 --> 00:11:20,642
and stretches out His hands to them,

218
00:11:20,662 --> 00:11:23,313
and blesses them and their sinful
mothers...

219
00:11:23,333 --> 00:11:27,381
And the mothers of these children all
stand there too, to one side,

220
00:11:27,401 --> 00:11:30,416
and weep; each one recognises her own boy
or girl,

221
00:11:30,436 --> 00:11:32,723
and they fly up to them and kiss them,

222
00:11:32,743 --> 00:11:36,973
wipe away their tears with their little
hands and beg them not to cry,

223
00:11:36,993 --> 00:11:39,280
because it is so good for them here...

224
00:11:41,800 --> 00:11:43,770
And down below, in the morning,

225
00:11:43,790 --> 00:11:48,071
the yard-keepers found the small body of
the boy who had run in and

226
00:11:48,091 --> 00:11:51,794
frozen behind the firewood; and they found
his mama too...

227
00:11:51,814 --> 00:11:56,031
She had died even before him; the two met
again at the Lord God's,

228
00:11:56,051 --> 00:11:56,673
in heaven.

229
00:11:57,443 --> 00:11:59,331
And why did I make up such a story,

230
00:11:59,351 --> 00:12:01,838
so out of place in an ordinary sensible
diary,

231
00:12:01,858 --> 00:12:03,419
and a writer's diary at that?

232
00:12:03,439 --> 00:12:06,308
And I had promised stories chiefly about
real events!

233
00:12:06,328 --> 00:12:07,399
But that is just it,

234
00:12:07,419 --> 00:12:11,105
it keeps seeming and appearing to me that
all this could really have

235
00:12:11,125 --> 00:12:14,812
happened, I mean what went on in the
cellar and behind the firewood;

236
00:12:14,832 --> 00:12:16,557
but as for the tree at Christ's,

237
00:12:16,577 --> 00:12:20,372
I really do not know how to tell you
whether it could have happened or

238
00:12:20,392 --> 00:12:22,771
not. That is why I am a novelist, to
invent.
